The exercise sends custom browser events using new Event(channel) and window.dispatchEvent. These are local signals, not network messages. Creating an event with new Event(channel) produces an event object whose type is the supplied channel string. Calling window.dispatchEvent(event) sends that object to matching window listeners, running their handlers before returning. It does not contact a server.

Try this standalone JavaScript example in a browser page:
```javascript
function receive() { console.log("Signal received"); }
window.addEventListener("lesson-ping", receive);
const signal = new Event("lesson-ping");
console.log(window.dispatchEvent(signal));
window.removeEventListener("lesson-ping", receive);
```
It prints Signal received, then true. The matching listener runs during dispatch. A true result means this event was not canceled, not that a network message arrived. Calling removeEventListener uses the same event name and function to remove the listener afterward; a later dispatch will no longer call receive.
